I've done quite a bit of research into  the PR tactics the sugar industry has  employed basically to downplay the link  between sugar and heart disease and  instead shift the blame to fat as the  culprit for heart disease and there is  now a new report that has released some  historical documents which date back to  the 1960s going all the way back to the  1960s the sugar industry has been  proactively trying to spin  misinformation around the connection  between sugar consumption and health and  it basically goes two different ways one  way that the sugar industry has been  able to do this is by funding studies  that ended up showing that heart disease  is linked to saturated fat so then the  focus becomes on low-fat diets and very  often when people change one element of  their diet it's relatively zero-sum and  a very easy way to get more calories  when you stop getting as many calories  from fat is to get more calories from  sugar the other way that the sugar  industry has done this and there's a  documentary which I recommended whose  name escapes me now but it's something  like is it sweet dreams I don't remember  but in any case it explores how the  sugar industry anytime an independent  study came out during the 60s 70s 80s  whenever saying sugar consumption is  being linked to a whole bunch of  different conditions and this can be  connected to pre-diabetes pre-diabetic  conditions and so many other things the  sugar industry would fund a study of its  own and they would never straight-up lie  and say there's no connection whatsoever  between consuming tons of sugar and  health effects negative health effects  but they would basically say it's  inconclusive and what it seems like is  you just generally want to control your  total calorie intake and this is the  idea that a calorie is a calorie whether  you get a calorie from sugar or whether  you get a calorie from whatever else  that as long as you're not eating too  many calories your health is going to be  unimpacted by what types of calories  you're getting now we  know that this is absolutely not true  and we can sort of think about this  conceptually by thinking if you look at  the calories that are in a spoonful of  sugar and I don't remember exactly what  number that is if you consider the  calories in a spoonful of sugar and the  amount of some other food you would have  to eat to get that number of calories  and in fact a good comparison would be  something like chicken breast or chicken  thigh you will be much more satisfied or  feeling full if you had that same number  of calories of chicken than if you  consume those calories as a spoonful of  sugar and this really goes to the heart  of the issue right that sugar is these  empty calories sugar is often used in  the form of high fructose corn syrup as  a filler we know corn is very very  heavily subsidized it's a really really  big racket and we now know that for 50  years the sugar industry has been  funding pseudoscience non science and  trying to shift the blame of the  connection between food and heart  disease off of sugar and to fat it's not  really a surprise I mean we've seen this  documented pretty well for a while this  does reveal some particular historical  records that are really really specific  and what what's really disturbing and  this is also revealed in that  documentary the name of which I just  cannot remember right now but it's very  very good it's on Netflix a Harvard  scientists were paid by the sugar  industry to do studies and again they  came out with very shockingly they're  just not able to say that there's any of  negative health repercussions of sugar  and we should all be aware of this we're  increasingly learning much more about  the fact that while we want to avoid  trans fats eating healthy fats is not  the worst thing for for for humans and  we're not talking about the Atkins diet  which does seem to have a fair share of  problems about just going all crazy and  saying my my my food intake is going to  be bacon and just a very processed meats  but that fat in and of itself is not as  big a problem as we thought  salt while you don't want to over  indulgence salt particularly if you have  a predisposition to high blood pressure  salt is not as bad as we were being told  it was for a long time but sugar refined  sugar really really is a major problem  so good that there's a major study about  this now that has revealed the the  specific and deliberate misinformation  campaign
 

